        <OtherAbstract Type="PIP" Language="eng">
            <AbstractText>Immediate accidents resulting from IUD insertion can be cardio-respiratory arrest, fortunately very infrequent, and uterine perforation. Secondary accidents include hemorrhage, the most frequent side effect, which happens in 25% of cases, and pain, in 10% of cases, and infection. This last complication happens only in 2-5% of cases, but in its several forms, endocervicitis, endometritis, salpingitis, pelvi-peritonitis, can lead to sterility, and, very rarely, to death. Intrauterine pregnancy with IUD in situ usually results in spontaneous abortion; the incidence of ectopic pregnancy in IUD wearers is 1/25 normal pregnancies. Disappearance of IUD strings can easily be checked by regular examination; in a few cases it is a sign of transuterine migration of the device, which can lead to complications.</AbstractText>
        </OtherAbstract>
